[direction] d-06 -> closed
**Completed — Extract quantitative literature benchmarks from the full texts already downloaded. Use them to compare the z=0 emulator slopes with X-COP and FLAMINGO, and to compute the Nelson et al. non-thermal correction at R500c. Also search once for documentation of the HACC profile mass selection and temperature weighting..** Partly done: the Nelson et al. benchmark is extracted, cited and applied, and FLAMINGO results were read from the full text. Three things remain open. (a) The FLAMINGO quotes were not added to references.bib with cite. (b) The search for HACC mass-selection and temperature-weighting documentation was not run, so q-01, q-03 and q-06 remain open. (c) The X-COP 1805.00042 slope comparison was not done. Dead end x-02 records (b) and (c).

(1) f-009: Nelson, Lau & Nagai 2014 (arXiv:1404.4636) is cited verbatim in /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-combo-cluster-profiles/runs/261020_143128/references.bib (tc-...-79). Their Eq. 7 is f_nth = 1 - A{1+exp[-((r/R200m)/B)^gamma]}, with A=0.452+-0.001, B=0.841+-0.008 and gamma=1.628+-0.019. It is accurate to about 10% over 0.1-1.5 R200m out to z=1.5, and it is redshift-universal when radius is scaled to R200m. I assumed R500c is about 0.5-0.6 R200m (about 0.5 at z=0, about 0.55-0.6 at z=0.5). That gives f_nth = 0.254 / 0.274 / 0.294 and 1/(1-f_nth) = 1.34 / 1.38 / 1.42 at x = 0.5 / 0.55 / 0.6. These values are hand arithmetic, not tool output.

(2) f-010: applying this correction to the emulator values M_HSE/M500 = 0.925 (z=0) and 0.840 (z=0.4987) from f-005 gives 1.24-1.31 and 1.16-1.19. That overcorrects by 16-31%. The f_nth needed to reconcile the emulator is about 0.075 at z=0 and 0.16 at z=0.4987, closer to the X-COP value of about 6% at R500. Confidence is low because the result depends on the T500 convention.

(3) f-011: from the FLAMINGO full text (2409.07849), Sec. 3.2-3.4 and Figs. 1, 2, 3 and 5:
- In the 1e13.75-1e14.25 Msun bin, all profile methods converge to b of about -0.1.
- The calibration assumed a constant b = -0.257.
- Fig. 2: volume-weighted profiles give a much smaller and less mass-dependent bias than X-ray-weighted ones, with mass-weighted profiles in between. FLAMINGO attributes most of the bias to X-ray weighting.
- Fig. 5: at fixed true mass, b(M) steepens with z (z = 0-1.5), while it stays almost unchanged at fixed M_HSE except at z=1.5.
- Values for each mass bin and redshift are only plotted, not tabulated, in the text I read.
- If the HACC CTP is mass-weighted, FLAMINGO implies a smaller bias than -0.2, which is consistent with the emulator's 0.075-0.16.
[usage]
[direction] d-05 -> closed
**Completed — Test whether the change in M_HSE/M500 between z=0 and z=0.4987 at the midpoint parameters is statistically robust. Cross-check s_n + s_T against the emulator's own pressure profile (CPP), propagate the T(R500)/T500 error, and restate the result in the FLAMINGO sign convention..** The redshift change in M_HSE/M500 is not statistically robust: it is about 1.2 sigma whichever way the slope is computed. I predicted CPP (P/P500) at the d-01 midpoint parameters (tc-...-62, -63). The files are CPP_z0.0000_prediction.csv and CPP_z0.4987_prediction.csv in /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-combo-cluster-profiles/runs/261020_143128/. The CGD, CTP and CPP bin values were read verbatim from the CSVs (tc-...-64 to -67, -69, -70). Slopes are centred finite differences over the same bin pairs as d-01 (0.365/0.649, 0.866/1.155 and 1.540/2.738 R500c), with GP errors treated as independent.

**Pressure-slope cross-check (f-012).** s_P from CPP compared with s_n+s_T from CGD and CTP:
- 0.49 R500, z=0: -1.981+-0.042 vs -2.133+-0.104, a 1.3 sigma difference.
- 0.49 R500, z=0.4987: -1.874+-0.088 vs -2.218+-0.117, 2.4 sigma.
- 1.00 R500, z=0: -3.052+-0.113 vs -3.128+-0.171, 0.37 sigma.
- 1.00 R500, z=0.4987: -3.232+-0.154 vs -3.243+-0.172, 0.05 sigma.
- 2.05 R500, z=0: -2.877+-0.042 vs -4.249+-0.086, 14 sigma.
- 2.05 R500, z=0.4987: -1.815+-0.207 vs -4.463+-0.125, 11 sigma.

CPP agrees with the density and temperature profiles at R500 but is much shallower beyond it. So at large radius CPP is not CGD x CTP, which points to a different averaging, weighting or halo sample (q-09).

**Mass ratio and bias (f-013).** T(R500)/T500, taken as the geometric mean of the two bins either side of R500, is 0.5916+-0.0047 at z=0 and 0.5181+-0.0101 at z=0.4987. With that error propagated:

| Slope used | z=0 M_HSE/M500 | z=0.4987 M_HSE/M500 | Difference | Significance |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| s_n+s_T | 0.925+-0.051 | 0.840+-0.048 | 0.085+-0.070 | 1.2 sigma |
| s_P (CPP) | 0.903+-0.034 | 0.837+-0.043 | 0.066+-0.055 | 1.2 sigma |

In the FLAMINGO convention, b = (M_HSE-M500)/M500:
- From s_n+s_T: b = -0.075+-0.051 at z=0 and -0.160+-0.048 at z=0.4987, so Delta b = -0.085+-0.070.
- From CPP: b = -0.097+-0.034 and -0.163+-0.043, so Delta b = -0.066+-0.055.

The bias becomes more negative with redshift, the same direction FLAMINGO finds for clusters, but the trend is not significant.

**Caveats:**
- The finite-difference errors assume the bins are independent; correlated GP errors would change them.
- The kT500 = G mu m_p M500/(2 R500) convention sets the absolute b values.
- The mass selection behind the profiles is undocumented (q-06). If it is a fixed mass threshold, part of the redshift trend could be a selection effect.

**Still open:** q-09, the origin of the CPP versus CGD x CTP mismatch beyond R500. No figures were made in this direction.
